          %h2#terminology Terminology
          %p
            A twisted arrow category is an alternative name for a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+factorisations"} category of factorisations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baues-Wirsching+cohomology"} Baues-Wirsching cohomology
            %em twisted arrow category
            is more often used in discussing Kan extensions and within the categorical literature
            %sup#fnref:1>
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            \.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The twisted arrow category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            a category is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
            of its
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi el
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi hom
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Tw(C) = el(hom) = * / hom
          %h3#explicit_description Explicit description
          %p
            Unpacking the well-known explicit construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+object"} comma objects
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cat}
            as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            has
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                objects:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                an arrow in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                morphisms: between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                are pairs of arrows
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
                such that the following diagram commutes:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi A
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo ←
                                %mi p
                            %mtd
                              %mi C
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi g
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi B
                            %mtd
                              %munder
                                %mo →
                                %mi q
                            %mtd
                              %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\begin{matrix}
                      A             &amp; \overset{p}{\leftarrow}   &amp; C            \\
                      f \downarrow  &amp;                           &amp; \downarrow g \\
                      B             &amp; \underset{q}{\to}         &amp; D
                      \\end{matrix}
              %p
                you could view then morphisms from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                as
                %em factorizations
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                through
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo =
                        %mi q
                        %mi f
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = q f p
          %p
            From the description above,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            is the same as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Arr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Arr(C)
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            above in the definition of morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %em reversed
          %h3#as_a_lax_colimit As a lax colimit
          %p
            The (opposite of the) category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            can be described as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limit#lax"} lax colimit
            of the diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi c
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to Cat\colon c\mapsto C/c
          %h2#example Example
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a partially ordered set, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            is isomorphic to the set of nonempty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} intervals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi c
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b]=\{c\in C| a\leq c\leq \b\}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\leq b
            in C ordered by inclusion (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#JT99"} Johnstone 1999
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              From its definition as a comma category, there is a functor (a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+opfibration"} discrete opfibration
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Tw
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\pi_C \colon Tw(C) \to C^{op} \times C
              %p which at the level of objects forgets the arrows:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\pi_C(f \colon A \to B) = (A,B)
              %p and keeps everything at the morphisms level.
          %h3#_and_wedges
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            and wedges
          %p
            One could say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            %em classifies
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge"} wedges
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon C^{op} \times C \to B
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extranatural+transformation"} extranatural transformations
              of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo •
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \stackrel{\bullet}{\to} F
          %p are the same as
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformations
              of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \to F \circ \pi_C
          %p
            This can be used to give a proof of the reduction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"} ends
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conical+limit"} conical limits
            in the
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Set}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ends+in+a+derivator"} ends in a derivator
          %p
            In particular, there is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op} \times C, B] \to [Tw(C), B]
            sending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon C^{op} \times C \to B
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar F \colon Tw(C) \to B
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi B
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi lim
                    %mover
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{b \in B} F(b, b) \cong lim\bar F
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The twisted arrow category is a special case of a
                %strong category of judgments
                in the sense of (
                %a{:href => "#MZ15"}> Melliès-Zeilberger 15
                ).
            %li
              %p
                The construction generalizes to
                = succeed "," do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                    \-category theory
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/%28infinity%2C1%29-category-theory"} ?
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+arrow+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} twisted arrow (∞,1)-category
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+factorizations"} category of factorizations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+arrow+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} twisted arrow (∞,1)-category
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#BN00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marta+Bunge"}> M. Bunge
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Susan+Niefield"} S. Niefield
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Exponentiability and single universes
                %strong 148
                (2000) pp.217-250.
            %li
              %p
                L. Errington,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Twisted Systems
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.34.6369"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Note on Discrete Conduché Fibrations
                %strong 5
                no.1 (1999) pp.1-11. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/1999/n1/n1.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Linton"}> Fred Linton
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Autonomous categories and duality of functors
                %strong 2
                no.3 (1965) pp.315-349.
            %li#DAGX
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Derived Algebraic Geometry X: Formal Moduli Problems
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories for the Working Mathematician
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul-Andr%C3%A9+Melli%C3%A8s"}> Paul-André Melliès
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noam+Zeilberger"} Noam Zeilberger
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Isbell Duality for Refinement Types
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1501.05115"} arXiv:1501.05115
                (2015).
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twisted_diagonal_(category_theory)"} Twisted diagonal (category theory)
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
                  (‘Equality in hyperdoctrines’, 1970) uses the term ‘twisted
                  %em morphism
                  category’.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
